WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.580
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:02.780
and

3
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.620
this is track three of the LOOP-R

4
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.640
course.

5
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.440
Today, when you ask AI for a

6
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.700
proposal and, in the same conversation,

7
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.440
ask whether it turned out well,

8
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.780
it always says yes.

9
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.800
The person who does the work never

10
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.480
evaluates their own work,

11
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.360
not on your team and not in

12
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.680
the loop.

13
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.000
In this track,

14
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.540
you'll meet the loop assistants,

15
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.020
each with just one role.

16
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.220
First,

17
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.940
the ones who do the work,

18
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.740
count, and diagnose.

19
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.800
Then, the ones who propose,

20
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.880
test, and judge.

21
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.540
Next, the Guardian.

22
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.500
And finally, Memory.

23
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.140
Each lesson has a short exercise:

24
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.360
when I ask you to,

25
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.480
pause and try it with one of

26
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.660
your real processes.

27
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.650
By the end,

28
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.540
you'll have written answer three on the

29
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.860
loop worksheet: what never changes without you.

30
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.380
The loop splits the cycle among nine

31
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.420
assistants, each with just one role.

32
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.560
Think of an employee who does one

33
00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:58.180
thing well.

34
00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.260
Each assistant reads only what its role

35
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.720
requires, writes only that role’s result,

36
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.940
and refuses anything outside it.

37
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.500
It sounds bureaucratic.

38
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.080
In fact, it’s the only way for

39
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.480
AI not to convince itself that it

40
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.000
improved.

41
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.820
Think of a support manager at a

42
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.220
small software company.

43
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.920
One person answers tickets;

44
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.680
another counts how many were reopened.

45
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.520
If it were the same person,

46
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.640
the reopening count would never go up,

47
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.960
because nobody likes counting their own mistakes.

48
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:24.740
In the loop,

49
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.880
the AI that writes the reply is

50
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.920
not the one that counts the result.

51
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.720
The one who does the work does

52
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.360
not measure; the one who measures does

53
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.560
not explain;

54
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.220
the one who explains does not propose.

55
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.200
The first is the Executor.

56
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.800
It is the only one that produces

57
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.540
the actual deliverable:

58
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.040
the proposal, the customer reply,

59
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:45.780
the campaign.

60
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.440
It follows the official version’s script exactly

61
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.400
and records one row in the spreadsheet:

62
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.180
date, version, and what came out.

63
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.820
It never changes its own script or

64
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.200
ignores what must never happen.

65
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.680
And unlike a chat,

66
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.080
it does not evaluate its work or

67
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.140
suggest improvements.

68
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.880
It just executes.

69
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.160
Think of a real estate agent receiving

70
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.340
leads from the portal.

71
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.600
The Executor replies to each lead using

72
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.520
the version one script and records the

73
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.160
date, version, property,

74
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.980
neighborhood, and time to reply.

75
00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.840
If the agent asks it to reply

76
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.420
better, it refuses.

77
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.180
“Better” is not an instruction;

78
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:23.980
it is a verdict,

79
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.080
and another assistant gives it.

80
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.160
The second is the Observer.

81
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.260
It turns the spreadsheet rows into evidence.

82
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.780
It counts, groups,

83
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.320
and checks whether the rows are complete.

84
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.240
If a column is missing or a

85
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.160
row is duplicated,

86
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.520
it writes “inconsistent data” and stops the

87
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.800
cycle there.

88
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.780
It never explains the cause,

89
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.300
proposes a change,

90
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.760
or uses information from outside the spreadsheet.

91
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.780
Its answer is a table with numbers

92
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:52.640
and counts.

93
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.900
A number without the count behind it

94
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.460
is not evidence;

95
00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.160
it is an impression.

96
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.733
The support manager’s company received

97
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:02.240
ten thousand tickets that year.

98
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.140
The Observer reports that thirty-seven percent concern

99
00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.340
the same issue,

100
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.840
the average response time,

101
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.080
and how many were reopened within seven

102
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:10.700
days.

103
00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.360
It does not say the product has

104
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.300
a serious defect.

105
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.220
That would already be an explanation.

106
00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.480
The third is the Critic.

107
00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.860
It reads the Observer’s table and the

108
00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.260
current script, then says what works,

109
00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.380
what fails, and where there is waste,

110
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.300
always with the number that proves it.

111
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.020
Each claim gets a strength:

112
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.100
strong, moderate, or weak.

113
00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.340
Weak claims go into “observed,

114
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.100
inconclusive.” And it never proposes.

115
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.180
The course example is a beauty clinic

116
00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.220
sending proposals over WhatsApp,

117
00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.700
using simulated data.

118
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.160
The owner reported a three percent conversion

119
00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:43.660
rate.

120
00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.580
The Critic reviewed two hundred proposals:

121
00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.107
observed conversion was

122
00:03:49.107 --> 00:03:50.493
two point five percent,

123
00:03:50.493 --> 00:03:51.900
five out of two hundred.

124
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.093
And

125
00:03:53.093 --> 00:03:54.900
eighty-three percent received

126
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.520
no reply within

127
00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.900
forty-eight hours.

128
00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.140
Before, the owner felt Tuesday’s proposals got

129
00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.040
more replies.

130
00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.700
Now a discrepancy had surfaced that nobody

131
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:05.620
had noticed.

132
00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.320
Now it’s your turn.

133
00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.140
Pause the video and set aside about

134
00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:09.640
ten minutes.

135
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.780
Choose a real proposal sent in the

136
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.320
last month and note what happened afterward,

137
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.640
using numbers, even small ones.

138
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.645
For example: forty sent,

139
00:04:18.645 --> 00:04:20.820
seven replied, one sale.

140
00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.180
The lesson materials include a ready-made prompt

141
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.460
to paste into your AI chat.

142
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.320
It’s like an instruction sheet for a

143
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.400
new employee: its role is diagnosis,

144
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.920
and it proposes nothing.

145
00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.740
You only replace the two marked sections.

146
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.320
Check the answer:

147
00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.280
three sections, a strength for each claim,

148
00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.860
and at least one in “observed,

149
00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.060
inconclusive.”

150
00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.960
If it includes a suggestion,

151
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.940
ask it to remove that and redo

152
00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.180
only the diagnosis.

153
00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.500
Then underline only the strong claims.

154
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.320
Those are worth testing.

155
00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.240
The most common kind of change in

156
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.860
a small company starts like this:

157
00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.200
someone has an idea,

158
00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.980
tests it for two weeks,

159
00:04:58.980 --> 00:04:59.940
feels it worked,

160
00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.500
and turns it into a rule.

161
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.060
The loop separates the idea,

162
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.380
the test, and the verdict into three

163
00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.360
different hands.

164
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.600
The first is the Optimizer.

165
00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.340
It reads the diagnosis and writes hypotheses

166
00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.300
in a fixed form:

167
00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.860
if there is one change,

168
00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.340
then this metric should move from this

169
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.720
value to that value,

170
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.320
because of this evidence.

171
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.980
One change per hypothesis,

172
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:22.640
never two.

173
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.140
And anything already discarded does not come

174
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.080
back.

175
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.060
The agent got two numbers from the

176
00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.240
Critic: seventy percent of leads never reply

177
00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.260
to the first message,

178
00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.840
and the average response takes three hours.

179
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.580
The hypothesis: if the first reply goes

180
00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.340
out within fifteen minutes,

181
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:41.580
the response rate should rise from thirty

182
00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:42.480
to

183
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.600
forty percent.

184
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.340
The second hand is the Experimenter.

185
00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.120
It designs the test:

186
00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.660
A is the current version,

187
00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.420
and B is the version with the

188
00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:51.920
change.

189
00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.520
Before anything is sent,

190
00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.620
it calculates the sample size needed for

191
00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.580
proof,

192
00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.480
so the difference is not just luck.

193
00:05:58.480 --> 00:05:59.400
It

194
00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:00.880
splits the work evenly,

195
00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.340
in order of arrival,

196
00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.900
and stops early only if a guardrail

197
00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:05.840
is breached.

198
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.080
Never because it is already winning.

199
00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:08.980
At the clinic,

200
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.780
the hypothesis that a message of up

201
00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.400
to eighty words would

202
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.220
raise replies from seventeen to twenty-two percent

203
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:17.420
required

204
00:06:17.425 --> 00:06:20.600
nine hundred eighty-five proposals per side to

205
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.780
prove such a small lift,

206
00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:22.900
or forty weeks,

207
00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.660
above the sixteen-week ceiling.

208
00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.620
It did not reduce the number.

209
00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.120
It escalated to the owner,

210
00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.755
who chose to measure a worthwhile lift:

211
00:06:30.755 --> 00:06:32.900
one hundred sixty-six per side,

212
00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:33.820
seven weeks.

213
00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.960
The third hand is the Evaluator.

214
00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.600
It gives one of three possible answers.

215
00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.320
“Insufficient sample”:

216
00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.320
one side has not reached the minimum.

217
00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.700
“A stays”: B did not beat A

218
00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.180
by enough, or made something worse.

219
00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.840
“B won”:

220
00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.780
it beat A by enough and made

221
00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:52.840
nothing worse.

222
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.300
There is no “B seems better.” And

223
00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.440
“insufficient sample” is the most common answer.

224
00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.540
An honest loop spends many weeks saying,

225
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.880
“I don’t know yet.” In the clinic’s

226
00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.390
second cycle, the version of up to

227
00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:09.335
eighty words raised replies from nineteen point

228
00:07:09.335 --> 00:07:11.300
three to twenty-nine point seven percent,

229
00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.320
with

230
00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.360
three hundred proposals per side.

231
00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:15.870
And the Evaluator said:

232
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:19.240
“A stays.” Complaints were six versus one,

233
00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.320
and the worksheet said zero tolerance.

234
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.840
The rule was applied as written.

235
00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.220
Put it all together.

236
00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.500
The one who proposes does

237
00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.240
not test; the one who tests does

238
00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:31.040
not judge.

239
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.520
The person who convinced themselves had all

240
00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.320
three roles in the same hands.

241
00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.280
The loop does not ask you to

242
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:37.900
be neutral.

243
00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.780
It asks for someone else to be

244
00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.620
the judge.

245
00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.860
Pause for about eight minutes and practice

246
00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.160
with Marcos’s case.

247
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.760
He manages support at a software company.

248
00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.040
Tickets about not being able to export

249
00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.080
a report were reopened most often.

250
00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:54.620
He wrote a new reply with a

251
00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.420
video and told everyone to use it

252
00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:57.300
starting Monday.

253
00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.880
By Friday, three out of twenty-three had

254
00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.540
reopened, and before,

255
00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:02.760
it was much higher.

256
00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.540
The new reply became standard,

257
00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:06.320
and the old one was deleted.

258
00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:07.800
Write down who proposed,

259
00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.480
tested, and judged;

260
00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.760
what the Experimenter would require first;

261
00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.480
and what the Evaluator would say on

262
00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:14.680
Friday.

263
00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.120
Then check the answer key.

264
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.320
Every loop learns to cheat the goal

265
00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.080
unless someone says what is forbidden.

266
00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.560
More replies can turn into an irritating

267
00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:25.760
message.

268
00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:28.840
Faster support can turn into closing a

269
00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.840
ticket without solving the problem.

270
00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.460
That is why the Guardian exists.

271
00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.420
It reads hypotheses before any test and

272
00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:36.540
gives one verdict:

273
00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.380
approved or vetoed.

274
00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:41.500
Nobody revisits that decision within the cycle.

275
00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.820
It is not persuaded by an argument,

276
00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:45.800
does not approve with reservations,

277
00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.300
and does not suggest an alternative.

278
00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:49.240
When in doubt,

279
00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:50.160
it vetoes.

280
00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:51.240
Your

281
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.980
common sense comes in when you write

282
00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:53.560
the list.

283
00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:54.600
For the agent,

284
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.660
it vetoes promising loan approval.

285
00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:58.200
For the support manager,

286
00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.600
it vetoes closing a ticket before the

287
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:01.600
customer confirms.

288
00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:02.700
For the clinic,

289
00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.660
it vetoes promising a result or a

290
00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.220
timeline, even if conversion would go up.

291
00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:10.320
The Guardian’s first list is the invariants:

292
00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.640
what no hypothesis may touch.

293
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:13.920
They are not goals;

294
00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.440
they are boundaries.

295
00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:16.960
Safety, the law,

296
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.780
promises the company does not make,

297
00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:20.460
and people who asked not to be

298
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:21.060
contacted.

299
00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:22.560
A good invariant is short,

300
00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.400
concrete, and starts with “never.”

301
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.640
If it takes two sentences to explain,

302
00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.080
it is still an opinion.

303
00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.400
And you write it once,

304
00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:32.960
on the loop worksheet.

305
00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.920
The agent wrote three:

306
00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.480
never promise loan approval;

307
00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.460
never state the condo fee or property

308
00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.200
tax without checking the title;

309
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.720
never contact anyone who asked not to

310
00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.700
receive messages.

311
00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.240
A reply saying a loan simulation was

312
00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.780
approved never even reaches the test.

313
00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.520
The clinic has four,

314
00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.860
including never discounting more than ten percent.

315
00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.840
The

316
00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.740
second list is “without getting worse” measures.

317
00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.100
Every goal takes this form:

318
00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:02.440
increase X without making Y and Z

319
00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:03.640
worse.

320
00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:05.080
Without that escort,

321
00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.520
the loop raises X by the cheapest

322
00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.220
route, which almost always damages Y.

323
00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:13.020
The Guardian vetoes foreseeable harm beforehand,

324
00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.520
and the Evaluator rejects harm that actually

325
00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:16.720
occurred.

326
00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.080
In the clinic’s first cycle,

327
00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.800
the Optimizer proposed closing with the question:

328
00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:23.500
would you rather start this week or

329
00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:24.000
next?

330
00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.380
The evidence was strong,

331
00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.240
and the promise was to raise replies

332
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.180
from seventeen to twenty-five percent.

333
00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.400
The Guardian vetoed it.

334
00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.600
The question assumes a purchase and sets

335
00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.480
a deadline, creating predictable pressure on complaints

336
00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.020
and opt-out requests.

337
00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.040
A goal without an escort is an

338
00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:40.960
invitation:

339
00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.960
“stop sending me this” counts as a

340
00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:43.560
reply.

341
00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.760
Each “without getting worse” measure gets a

342
00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:46.640
number beside it:

343
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.220
the tolerance.

344
00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.100
Zero seems safest,

345
00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:52.120
but it is usually the wrong choice,

346
00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:54.960
because rare events fluctuate by chance.

347
00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.460
One complaint this month,

348
00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:57.660
six the next,

349
00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:58.980
with nothing changed.

350
00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:01.400
A rare event’s tolerance needs to sit

351
00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.180
above its normal fluctuation.

352
00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.220
For something that happens one percent of

353
00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:07.260
the time, with three hundred on each

354
00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:09.940
side, the fluctuation is about one percentage

355
00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:10.440
point.

356
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.952
So the tolerance becomes zero point zero

357
00:11:12.952 --> 00:11:13.452
one.

358
00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:14.850
That is what the clinic learned:

359
00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:16.580
six versus one out of three hundred

360
00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:17.580
is noise.

361
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.660
The owner did not reopen the verdict.

362
00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:21.960
She changed the tolerance for future tests.

363
00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.240
Even so, the Guardian vetoed the price

364
00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.700
right after the greeting.

365
00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.340
Tolerance is not permission.

366
00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:29.680
The third thing the Guardian watches is

367
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:31.560
the ceiling: the cost of AI per

368
00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.540
cycle and the number of tests running

369
00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:34.960
at once.

370
00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.600
Once the ceiling is reached,

371
00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:37.920
it approves nothing else,

372
00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.140
not even the perfect hypothesis.

373
00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.360
If one test is already running and

374
00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:43.400
the maximum is one,

375
00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.640
the next approved idea waits in the

376
00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:46.740
queue.

377
00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:47.840
At the clinic,

378
00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.780
the ceiling was fifty reais per weekly

379
00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:51.720
cycle and one test at a time.

380
00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.730
You will write your ceiling in track

381
00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:54.230
four.

382
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.340
Consider the support manager with six agents

383
00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.420
and eight hundred tickets a month.

384
00:11:58.420 --> 00:11:59.980
With three tests at once,

385
00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.840
each would get one hundred thirty tickets

386
00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.320
per side, and none would reach the

387
00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:05.840
sample size needed for proof.

388
00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.660
One at a time,

389
00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.280
each test finishes in weeks,

390
00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.580
not months.

391
00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:12.600
The ceiling does not limit improvement.

392
00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:13.960
It limits waste.

393
00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.820
Now write answer three on your loop

394
00:12:15.820 --> 00:12:16.480
worksheet.

395
00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.600
Pause for about twelve minutes and use

396
00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:20.220
the process you chose in track two.

397
00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.020
First, three invariants,

398
00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.620
each one sentence starting with “never,”

399
00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:26.860
concrete enough for a new employee to

400
00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.300
check without asking.

401
00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.340
Then

402
00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.260
two “without getting worse” measures that you

403
00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:33.240
record as numbers,

404
00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:34.360
such as complaints,

405
00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:36.520
margin, or opt-out requests.

406
00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.020
Put a tolerance beside each one.

407
00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:40.160
For a rare event,

408
00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:41.180
never use zero:

409
00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.040
start with zero point zero one.

410
00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.460
Finally, check the list against your company’s

411
00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.220
latest improvement idea.

412
00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.060
If it passes but you feel it

413
00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:50.180
should not,

414
00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:51.400
an invariant is missing.

415
00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.420
Then ask someone on your team whether

416
00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.760
they could check it without asking you.

417
00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.900
Your company has already tested dozens of

418
00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:59.760
things, and almost all of them were

419
00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:00.580
forgotten.

420
00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.100
Without memory, the loop does not learn;

421
00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:04.480
it rediscovers.

422
00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.000
Memory is the assistant that closes the

423
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:07.660
cycle.

424
00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:09.740
It reads everything the others wrote,

425
00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:11.780
records one row in the history,

426
00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:13.580
and puts each hypothesis in the right

427
00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:14.540
place.

428
00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:15.940
It never deletes,

429
00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.840
rewrites, or summarizes so much that the

430
00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:19.680
reason is lost.

431
00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:20.780
In the next cycle,

432
00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.560
the Critic checks what has already been

433
00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.100
resolved, and the Optimizer checks what has

434
00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:26.180
already been discarded.

435
00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.040
At the agent’s real estate agency,

436
00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.320
someone tested sending photos before the price

437
00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:31.900
in two thousand twenty-four,

438
00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:33.300
and it worked worse.

439
00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:34.760
Nobody recorded it.

440
00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.220
In two thousand twenty-six,

441
00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.040
a new agent repeated the test.

442
00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.660
The cost:

443
00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.580
one month of poorly answered leads to

444
00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:45.000
relearn what the company already knew.

445
00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:47.220
Every hypothesis ends up in one of

446
00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.580
three drawers.

447
00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:50.720
Learned: tested, won,

448
00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.440
and became the official version,

449
00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.560
with the number.

450
00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.920
Discarded: lost or was vetoed,

451
00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:56.760
with the reason,

452
00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.340
so it does not return in the

453
00:13:58.340 --> 00:13:59.060
same form.

454
00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:01.000
And observed, untested:

455
00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.460
the evidence was weak,

456
00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.200
so it waits for a larger sample.

457
00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:05.940
That third drawer is the most forgotten,

458
00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.180
and most valuable.

459
00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:09.580
It keeps the “seems like” ideas without

460
00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.540
turning them into rules or throwing them

461
00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:12.040
away.

462
00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:12.960
At the clinic,

463
00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:13.900
opening

464
00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:15.680
the proposal by mentioning one point from

465
00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.980
the client’s review went into learned:

466
00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.584
replies rose from nineteen point three to

467
00:14:20.584 --> 00:14:21.560
twenty-nine point three percent,

468
00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:23.320
and it became version two.

469
00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.920
The forced-choice question and the price after

470
00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.200
the greeting went into discarded.

471
00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:29.610
And be careful:

472
00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.740
twelve proposals do not make a rule.

473
00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:33.700
A hunch based on a few cases

474
00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.340
has its own drawer.

475
00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:36.500
Alongside the drawers,

476
00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:37.640
Memory keeps the history:

477
00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.500
one row for each event,

478
00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:40.180
in order.

479
00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:41.920
Date, cycle, hypothesis,

480
00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.140
verdict with a number,

481
00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:44.300
what was monitored,

482
00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.600
who decided, what happened to the version,

483
00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:47.500
and cost.

484
00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:48.760
No narrative.

485
00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:50.760
It is the history log with an

486
00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:52.680
undo button for your process.

487
00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:54.600
If version two gets worse,

488
00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.020
you return to version one,

489
00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:58.040
and the rollback becomes a new row.

490
00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:00.000
The promotion is not erased.

491
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.280
The support manager wrote his first row

492
00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:03.520
like this:

493
00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:04.680
before the loop,

494
00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:07.600
standard reply rewritten with a video;

495
00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:09.610
twenty-three tickets in one week,

496
00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.660
three reopened; no A side for comparison;

497
00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.060
decided by me;

498
00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:16.560
no test; old reply deleted.

499
00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.460
It is an honest record of a

500
00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:19.180
bad test.

501
00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.160
And it keeps the next manager from

502
00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:22.200
repeating it.

503
00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.200
Two companies use the same AI chat,

504
00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:25.000
the same model,

505
00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.620
and the same kind of script.

506
00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:27.620
After a year,

507
00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:30.200
what separates them is not the AI.

508
00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.180
It is what each

509
00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:34.620
one knows about its own customers:

510
00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:35.820
what it tried,

511
00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:37.960
what worked, what failed,

512
00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.780
and at what cost.

513
00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:42.380
The method calls this a learning advantage.

514
00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.040
The advantage becomes who learns faster from

515
00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:45.940
what they already do.

516
00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:48.060
Two real estate agencies in the same

517
00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:49.260
neighborhood:

518
00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.920
the agent’s agency has fourteen history rows,

519
00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.600
three script versions,

520
00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.940
five discarded hypotheses with numbers,

521
00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:58.820
and one recorded rollback.

522
00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:00.970
The other has a script someone improved

523
00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:03.160
three times without recording it.

524
00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:05.060
If it copies the agent’s script,

525
00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:06.460
it gets version three.

526
00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:08.240
But it does not get the five

527
00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.560
ideas already known not to work.

528
00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.920
Last exercise on this track.

529
00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.120
Pause for about ten minutes and write

530
00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.320
the first row in your loop history.

531
00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:19.460
Choose a real change that has already

532
00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:21.680
happened in the process on your worksheet:

533
00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.520
a text, a script,

534
00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:24.320
a rule.

535
00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:26.420
Note the approximate date,

536
00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:28.120
mark it as before the loop,

537
00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.880
and describe the change in one sentence.

538
00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:32.920
Then write what happened,

539
00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.240
with at least one number,

540
00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:36.100
and whether there was an A side

541
00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:36.920
for comparison.

542
00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.200
Next, who decided and what happened to

543
00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.800
the previous version.

544
00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.520
Finally, choose the drawer.

545
00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.220
It will usually be observed,

546
00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:47.200
untested,

547
00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:50.300
because almost nothing was truly tested before

548
00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:51.220
the loop.

549
00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:53.500
Don’t remember the exact number?

550
00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:54.640
Write an approximation.

551
00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:55.580
Then

552
00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:58.040
ask your team what they already tried

553
00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.620
that did not work.

554
00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:01.780
Each answer becomes a row.

555
00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:04.600
The loop works because each assistant has

556
00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:05.580
just one role.

557
00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:07.900
The Executor does the work and records

558
00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:10.160
it; the Observer counts;

559
00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:13.140
the Critic diagnoses and labels the strength

560
00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:14.200
of the evidence.

561
00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:16.500
The Optimizer proposes one change at a

562
00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:19.160
time, the Experimenter does the math first,

563
00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.880
and the Evaluator gives only three answers.

564
00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:23.860
The Guardian applies the list you wrote,

565
00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:25.440
without negotiation.

566
00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:28.340
And Memory keeps everything in three drawers

567
00:17:28.340 --> 00:17:30.740
and a history that is never erased.

568
00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:32.360
You leave with answer three on the

569
00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.020
worksheet and the first row in the

570
00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:34.560
history.

571
00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:36.100
One assistant remains:

572
00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:38.440
the Meta-agent, which looks at the history

573
00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:39.800
and only reports.

574
00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:40.820
In track four,

575
00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:42.560
you’ll meet it and answer the question

576
00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:45.820
every loop must answer before changing anything:

577
00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:47.460
how do we measure for real?

578
00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:48.600
Until then.
